<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Mongol invasion of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1240">1240</a>–<a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe">Mongol invasion of Europe</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The first Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was a raid by a Mongol army under Baidar and Orda in 1240–1241, covering the flank of the main invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked Sandomierz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and on 9 April 1241 defeated the Silesian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>, where he was killed. They then withdrew south to join Batu in Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-mongol-invasion-of-poland.jpg" alt="First Mongol invasion of Poland" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown / (of the reproduction) Wroclaw University Library / T. Zoltowska-Huszcza (sorry for ignoring diacritics) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HedwigManuscriptLiegnitz_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with three armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a>, the general in charge, did not want Polish forces to threaten the flank of the main invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and gave one army the task of keeping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, and the military orders, from helping King Béla IV. A reconnaissance expedition under Baidar, Kadan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda Khan</a> began scouting in late 1240, and a detachment advancing from Ruthenia through Volodymyr-Volhynskyi entered the Duchy of Sandomierz that winter.</p><p>The Polish forces in all far outnumbered the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> of 12,000–20,000 men sent against them, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> moved fast, attacked from several directions before the Polish armies could unite, and defeated them one by one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed Sutiejsk, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> and took Sandomierz after its surrender on 13 February, then split. Orda's forces devastated central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as far north as Łęczyca before turning via Sieradz towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. Baidar and Kadan defeated the army of Włodzimierz, voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, at Tursko and another army at Chmielnik on 18 March, and Kraków, abandoned by its citizens in panic, was seized and burned by 24 March.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> gathered his forces and allies around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>, even abandoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and waited for his brother-in-law Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Hearing that the Bohemians were days away, the Mongols broke off the siege of Wrocław to catch Henry first. On 9 April a charge of knights drew Henry's cavalry away from its infantry; a smokescreen hid the knights as they were surrounded and killed, and the rest of the army was encircled. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-jackson">Peter Jackson</a> the only military order at the battle was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, perhaps 68–88 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> did not take Legnica Castle, but they plundered Silesia before joining their main forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, which they defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Battle of Mohi</a>, while the Bohemians stayed at home. The death of the Grand Khagan Ögedei and quarrels between Batu, Guyuk and Buri then drew the princes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, which probably saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from being overrun.</p><p>Henry's death set back the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, which came only in the 14th century, and meant the loss of Silesia, which drifted towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>. Settlements such as Sutiejsk were never rebuilt. Larger Mongol invasions of Poland followed in 1259–1260 and 1287–1288.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Mongol invasion of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
